# Benchmarking VQLS on linear systems from an ADMM Max-Cut solver

## 1. What this study does

VQLS is not applied to Max-Cut. It is applied to *one linear solve* sitting
inside an iteration of the classical optimizer that solves Max-Cut. The chain:

```
Max-Cut on G          NP-hard combinatorial problem
  -> SDP relaxation   max (1/4)<L,X>  s.t.  diag(X) = 1, X >= 0
  -> ADMM             first-order splitting method
  -> x-update         a linear system  Q z = b        <-- VQLS goes here
```

An ADMM run calls that solve hundreds of times. Replacing it with VQLS means
replacing the *inner* solver, not the optimizer. Any end-to-end claim must
account for the outer loop.

See `vqls_admm_pipeline.tex` for the diagram.

## 2. The operator

The ADMM `x`-update solves the saddle-point system

```
Q = [ rho*I   A^T ]        dim = n^2 + n
    [ A       0   ]
```

with `A` encoding `diag(X) = 1`. Three properties decide the whole study:

**`Q` is indefinite.** Still Hermitian, so `BaseVQLS` accepts it, but there is
no SPD structure to exploit and no positive-definite preconditioning story.

**`kappa(Q) = phi^2 ~ 2.618` for every `n`.** With `A A^T = I` the spectrum is
`{rho}` with multiplicity `n^2 - n`, plus `(rho +- sqrt(rho^2+4))/2`. At
`rho = 1` that gives `{1, 1.618, -0.618}`. Conditioning is therefore *removed
as a variable* — unusual, and the main reason this operator is a clean test bed.

**`Q` does not depend on the graph or on the iteration.** Only `b` changes.
The Pauli decomposition is computed once and reused across every graph and
every ADMM step. This is the single structural argument in VQLS's favour here,
since the decomposition is exponential in the qubit count.

Contrast with the IPM Schur operator (`ipm_schur_*.npz`), which is SPD and
smaller (`dim = n`) but graph-dependent, changes every Newton step, and has
conditioning that degrades by four orders of magnitude as the method converges.
Different study; see §7.

## 3. Interface requirements

Three adjustments stand between the ADMM output and `BaseVQLS`.

### 3.1 Dimension padding

`dim(Q) = n^2 + n` is not a power of two (`n=5` gives 30). Pad with an identity
block:

```
[ Q  0 ] [ z ]   [ b ]
[ 0  I ] [ y ] = [ 0 ]
```

The blocks decouple, so `z` is unchanged. **The RHS padding must be zero** —
not for correctness of `z` (decoupling already guarantees that) but because
VQLS returns a *normalized* state. Any nonzero padding component would consume
amplitude and corrupt `z` after renormalization.

| graph `n` | `dim Q` | padded | qubits `N` |
|---|---|---|---|
| 5  | 30  | 32  | 5 |
| 10 | 110 | 128 | 7 |

Note how fast this grows: 7 qubits buys a 10-node graph.

### 3.2 Normalization of `b`

`BaseVQLS` passes `b` directly to `qiskit.circuit.library.StatePreparation`,
which rejects a non-unit vector. Normalize and retain `||b||`.

### 3.3 Scale recovery

VQLS returns a unit-norm `|x>` defined up to global phase — it solves for the
*direction* of `x`. The magnitude is re-fitted classically by least squares:

```
alpha = <Q x_hat, b> / ||Q x_hat||^2
```

This is an `O(N^2)` host-side matrix-vector product and is **not** part of the
quantum routine. State it explicitly in any resource accounting.

## 4. Cost functions

With the LCU decomposition `Q = sum_l a_l P_l`:

```
Global:  C_G = 1 - |<b|psi>|^2 / <psi|psi>

Local:   C_L = 1/2 - (1/2N) * sum_j <psi| B Z_j B^dag |psi> / <psi|psi>
```

where `|psi> = Q|x(theta)>` and `B|0> = |b>`. Both reach 0 at the solution.

**They are not numerically comparable.** At identical parameters they return
different values because they are different objectives descending different
landscapes. A smaller `final_cost` under `C_L` does not mean a better answer
than a larger one under `C_G`. Compare recovered-solution metrics only.

`C_L` exists to mitigate barren plateaus, which appear at large qubit counts
with random initialization. At `N <= 7` that regime is not reached, so `C_L`
carries its overhead without delivering its benefit — expect `C_G` to match or
win at these sizes, and do not generalize that finding upward.

## 5. Benchmark metrics

### 5.1 Report these two, always together

| metric | definition | fails when |
|---|---|---|
| residual | `\|Qx - b\| / \|b\|` | `kappa` large — small residual with large error |
| fidelity | `\|<x_exact\|x_hat>\|^2` | near the optimum — quadratic, saturates at 1.0000 |

Neither is safe alone. The error bound is

```
||x - x*|| / ||x*||  <=  kappa(Q) * residual
```

so at `kappa = 4.2e3` a residual of `1.7e-2` bounds the error by ~72 — no
information. A measured case from `ipm_schur_n32` iteration 5: residual
`1.73e-2` (the best in the sweep) alongside fidelity `0.0887` (useless). The
residual was flattering a near-random answer.

For the ADMM operator `kappa = 2.618`, so residual is trustworthy here — but
report fidelity anyway so the comparison against IPM stays meaningful.

### 5.2 Report these as run conditions

| parameter | why it matters |
|---|---|
| ansatz depth `d` | the binding constraint at fixed `kappa`; see §6 |
| parameter count | `2Nd` for `HE_ansatz`, `Nd` for `fixed_ansatz` |
| `maxiter` | COBYLA budget; check whether it was exhausted |
| cost evaluations | if `== maxiter`, the run did not converge |
| seeds | **>= 10**; a single seed cannot separate `C_G` from `C_L` |
| `kappa(Q)` | the other axis of accuracy |
| `L` (Pauli terms) | measurement cost driver |
| `num_shots` | Sampler variants only |

Report seed spread as median `[min, max]`, never a single run.

### 5.3 Resource counters

For the shot-based Sampler solvers, the Hadamard-test loop costs

```
circuits per cost evaluation  =  N * L^2  +  L(L+1)/2
```

which is the honest measurement-overhead figure. Measured for dense random `A`:

| `N` | `L` | circuits / cost eval |
|---|---|---|
| 2 | 10  | 255 |
| 3 | 36  | 4,554 |
| 4 | 136 | 83,300 |
| 5 | 528 | 1,533,576 |

At 4096 shots each, `N=5` is ~6e9 shots per cost evaluation. Quote this; it is
the number that decides whether the method is deployable.

## 6. Known failure modes

**Ansatz expressibility is the binding constraint, not conditioning.** On the
ADMM operator (`kappa = 2.618`, about as good as it gets), `d=2` yields fidelity
~0.50 on a 32-dimensional space — no better than noise. Raising to `d=8` gives
~0.96. Parameter count `2Nd` grows linearly in `N` while the Hilbert space grows
exponentially, so depth must scale with `N`. Sweeping `d` at fixed `kappa` is
the cleanest experiment this operator supports.

**Sparsity in `A` does not save you if `b` is dense.** The Estimator solvers
build operator products in Pauli space. A dense `b` makes `StatePreparation`
generic, so `B_LCU` has all `4^N` terms — 1024 at `N=5`. The local cost then
assembles `A^dag B Z_j B^dag A` with `112^2 * 1024^2 ~ 1.3e10` terms before
simplification, and the global cost assembles `B^dag A V` with ~`1e8` terms,
rebuilt every cost evaluation. Both exhaust memory at `N=5`.

`SDPSystem.for_vqls()` supplies `A_LCU` but not `B_LCU`, so the shipped escape
hatch closes only half the door.

*Mitigation:* the Estimator path is an exact statevector simulation, so these
quantities can be computed with dense linear algebra at no loss of fidelity to
the model — `M = B^dag Q V` is a `2^N x 2^N` product. `fast_exact.py`
implements this and agrees with `vqls.py` to 5.6e-17.

**Padding must not leak into the RHS.** See §3.1.

## 7. Suggested experiments

1. **Depth sweep** — `d` in `{2,4,6,8,10,12}`, ADMM operator, >=5 seeds.
   Isolates expressibility at fixed conditioning. Primary result.
2. **Cost comparison** — `C_G` vs `C_L`, >=10 seeds, at the depth where the
   solver actually works. Below that depth the comparison is meaningless.
3. **Conditioning sweep** — IPM operator only, `kappa` from 1.16 to 4.2e3 at
   fixed qubit count. The ADMM family cannot support this (`kappa` is constant),
   and it is the sharpest available demonstration of where VQLS breaks.
4. **Shot noise** — Sampler variants at `N<=3`, several `num_shots` values.
   Report circuits per evaluation alongside accuracy.

## 8. Scope

Simulation and proof-of-concept only. `get_LCU` and `Operator(circuit)` scale
exponentially in `N`; the shipped systems reach 7 qubits. Scale recovery is
classical. VQLS has no proven advantage over classical linear solvers for these
systems, and this study does not claim one — it characterizes where the method
fails on operators a real optimizer actually produces.

All shipped systems use one graph (`p = 0.5`, `seed = 1`, `rho = 1`). For the
IPM family the graph affects the operator; for the ADMM family it provably does
not.

